Implement one sparse Polynomial<A: Coefficient>, with Poly=Polynomial<C> and BigPoly=Polynomial<BigC>. Preserve legacy constructors, operators, eval Result<A,String>, display, coefficient conjugation, and Ratio domain/signatures. Clone coefficients, never require Copy. Expose terms for adapters. Implement try_constant, try_var, try_add, try_sub, try_mul, try_neg, try_pow, try_eval with mutable Budget and typed errors. Validate all inputs even for zero/exponent-zero; charge work; preflight term/degree/bit/byte growth before allocations. Defaults 8192 terms/64 degree. Legacy infallible methods/operators use documented expect compatibility wrappers. Allocation OOM is outside contract. Add fallible Ratio residual/eval preserving nonzero denominator. No separate Big implementation.
Legacy wrappers MUST use effectively unlimited operations, input bytes and coefficient bits, retaining old 8192 terms/64 degree and scalar overflow panic compatibility; caller-budget limits apply to try_* APIs.
The checked try_var API accepts impl AsRef<str>, checks borrowed UTF-8 byte length before to_owned; legacy var retains impl Into<String>. InputBytes means aggregate UTF-8 symbol bytes across every variable occurrence in one polynomial (repeated names count repeatedly), separately for each input/intermediate/output; evaluation-map names are aggregated separately. This is not combined live heap use or cumulative allocation accounting. Validate aggregates before cloning, and preflight output aggregate growth before cloning new monomial keys. Work counters count logical algebra/validation steps, not individual string bytes. Zero/exponent-zero shortcuts must not skip aggregate validation.
Reject known resource failures before owned key allocation. Reserve constructor/insertion logical work before cloning or formatting keys. Metric encoding must use a shared preflighted variable builder with no second constructor charge. Negation computes checked coefficient negation before cloning its key. Budgets are finite deterministic logical charges, not allocator/heap/CPU guarantees. Test large-key failure paths with thread-local allocation-size observation.
